  "notes": [
    "[GENERAL] Phoenix oolong (Fenghuang Dancong) originates from the Phoenix Mountain (Fenghuang Shan) area of Guangdong, China, where leaves are traditionally picked from mature, semi-wild tea trees rather than low-pruned bushes.",
    "[GENERAL] Dancong oolongs are renowned for naturally mimicking intense floral, honeyed, and stone-fruit scents through specialized harvesting and processing, without any added artificial or natural flavorings.",
    "[GENERAL] For traditional Gongfu brewing, use water just off the boil (200°F–212°F / 95°C–100°C) with a high leaf ratio (about 5–7 grams per 100–120 ml of water) and very quick 10- to 15-second steeps to draw out aroma without extracting bitterness.",
    "[GENERAL] For standard Western-style pot brewing, steep approximately 2 to 3 grams (about 1 rounded teaspoon) per 8 ounces of water at 195°F–205°F for 2 to 3 minutes.",
    "[GENERAL] Whole-leaf Phoenix oolongs are highly durable and can be re-infused 6 to 10 or more times, shifting in flavor and aromatic profile with each subsequent pour."
  ],
